nce/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/5fdd95564252a9a4218055ca7670a535233aa026955b7e410700e26c4f7d170c.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/1f42d4df263db3845ca2361b8f89c4075e0cdb9b39e5564c39ae82115932a201.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/874fc16afdc829b59ebb3f871e48722f6a11e475577e3195549d68cb2c836d30.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/5765a85c90552570f9a2a041f2f324a9fa0ce183d42708344531a13fbee802c8.png)\n\nConsequently,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/ee7def88d1be490478913a454811c3e0dd4ea0f3c93d2bff532d1d112d270e51.png)\n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/f37c2a4dfc6f3d9d7a3b80b6001b45bb42f00969109be97a94c018d4e69b89a0.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/5c4c54de5cbe2051d295c0e7dd0128d4eb7fedc8a82b7aa3b93d871238bb60d7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{4}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/c6714d0a6eaa5c7d354d46e8d6d60da7f3d2011df0fc75773036cf7ae510c5e3.png), find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/24878591902884f45f14f5c447bc326586a411a8870b205fc12825a4ab2c738a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/5c644b15efcf5a4274de23242a722604ead2ff800ea29ba7a650729227fefdf0.png) in terms of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/a7b9033c421072576aaf959980d778c319a8ed0f849e08db644b63bc6994088c.png) we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/166c90a9c29f99347bff6b397f31d705ec6407a19b4d71246f98308bbd7a5473.png) we want to match the expression\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/fc79367916dd7671c17de4f40b3d83f91d41c2f9988645964de0b53e5b11ab9b.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/91f686c6ca02e85c386cb0f9e80b66beface446d3a541952e0ac91738ad0db37.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/d54b9a8919c7b6106fcf5770c62b1823605d7be98a55d5333e73bcfb7528997a.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/6e1bbbe9eb66856824ff70d16cc2c077519d361546b226a67146e20f15a24dd0.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/f1c944e6afa4e9cd84bc68004a2bfd918d81498a727aeb7c56ec627806bf93f6.png), we have:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/b7ac390c4e43b2fa2fbb4063632f98869256cea877caa5e5dad4aad33ae