"2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244913} {:answer/id #uuid "5022f32c-324b-5430-acb8-1b1c5bfc7fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244914} {:answer/id #uuid "817bc788-aa3f-5cce-a585-dd99a0106a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244915} {:answer/id #uuid "f7699e4a-c740-55bf-968c-bde2e1328b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244916} {:answer/id #uuid "24d6b208-43b8-5d3e-88a3-189b1a4b59f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24d6b208-43b8-5d3e-88a3-189b1a4b59f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244917} :answer-field/id #uuid "989889d2-e847-571d-b93a-933db73337d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a11bc4a2-fd7a-5333-a516-b057f55e0ba3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120254" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/6882323d2c8017e95100a0ab9d1091fdf149e884ef27d2d2bf59ab7609b1a960.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/a8309dc45ac43c6642010e744de5a062d6575d48ba85bd642df39dd261f47422.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=-2a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc2ca1ac-baf7-50c5-9c60-57a1ed1533c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244920} {:answer/id #uuid "d4141f81-6004-5a81-ae43-db9641fafd45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244921} {:answer/id #uuid "55438839-19ba-5f55-be85-a1bbb8ca93ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244922} {:answer/id #uuid "18f316b1-e2ff-53ed-a4d7-e3aa849442f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244923} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc728fe-2da8-5566-a283-1e201b0de756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc2ca1ac-baf7-50c5-9c60-57a1ed1533c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244920} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaab8ddf-1251-539a-9910-4a883dc533e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d3394e1-e3ca-5fef-81fe-c269ab4905cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-120257" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/551316d1e36175ca302c5c472d4109a9bc9cadf32cc85d2a1751424a386070cc.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/40a0137d6227212db9b7ef9f349029838f3f51398790ec5e1c304e12c3743a0f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{1}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a687ab2-ea90-5824-9ba5-4ed8ae14f802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310723} {:answer/id #uuid "63c0c53b-c2d7-5f06-bdd1-d1443d16baac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310724} {:answer/id #uuid "9ace3fd4-7686-501e-9cab-130766debd44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310725} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0a54b2-2c7a-558c-887a-0a1efb1bc99d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310726} {:answer/id #uuid "294d056a-3875-53ac-ae2b-b67f67226abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ace3fd4-7686-501e-9cab-130766debd44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310725} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7629b1f-33ae-51af-8a50-8de25ffe6a15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c6d0a6c5-043c-5c21-9b3d-b465d593098a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120260" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/73e0ac67304b5e7682abddb59fcd01a9e8aca88831a303bf5b31a72ff772228d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-3b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/2484cf9fdda85c5c516babe6814f34c369ebdd91d6f100f0ce0fe4102e3c90a6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-3b=-3a-3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c2a97e3-e0a9-5f32-bcd1-1aa110198dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310730} {:answer/id #uuid "00b326a5-5d20-5dbe-ad5f-602a0161eade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310731} {:answer/id #uuid "f76afb29-4f8b-5c3d-ac84-11f2c034d54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310732} {:answer/id #uuid "ed01b7ff-cbb3-57f4-9fda-cbc71952ee9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310733} {:answer/id #uuid "c47cdc39-faa1-5a79-acfc-b375a31d9ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f76afb29-4f8b-5c3d-ac84-11f2c034d54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310732} :answer-field/id #uuid "648263a5-2cb8-5739-b310-df939d97e60a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "579d00a6-a778-595a-a8fe-d160cda52449" :question/math-academy-id "q-120279" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/fc7d37da99173b7fa99f2f5f0bbf1478a54505628ed827488a18b3d01535f81f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/6c9273c0b89d4c06c8a8591b8e8e94b0c83b2d1d4bd04b64ed8cb0a185619b5f.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=2|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2k.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-j-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-j-2k.$\n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed5bc7a0-60e9-501d-84bb-229c657a63b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{1}{2}h+\\frac{1}{2}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310737} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bc1f90-65f7-5311-bdee-98bcc5bcbdb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{4}h+\\frac{1}{4}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310738} {:answer/id #uuid "7c796899-7c91-5113-96b1-20fca7828264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{5}{8}h+\\frac{3}{8}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310739} {:answer/id #uuid "be17862a-48e0-5b2f-a69b-dd7014b0f082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{1}{8}h+\\frac{3}{4}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310740} {:answer/id #uuid "9575391e-2027-5cdf-a37a-7d393201587e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{8}h+\\frac{5}{8}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9575391e-2027-5cdf-a37a-7d393201587e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{8}h+\\frac{5}{8}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310741} :answer-field/id #uuid "3164c9bf-3cc8-5754-9865-06be6104b342" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0b691a2-9da7-5f9d-b601-8f5daf61f352" :question/math-academy-id "q-120304" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/6560a7f05ae95638cbfe902dfa022b2d8de6b07c6e1e6f85051ccf87b92ec338.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OI}=i$ and $J$ divides $\\overset{―}{HI}$ in the ratio $HJ:JI=5:3.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OHI,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\overset{\\to }{HO}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-h+i \\\\  & =i-h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $HJ:JI=5:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{HJ}=\\frac{5}{8}\\overset{\\to }{HI}=\\frac{5}{8}(i-h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ} \\\\  & =h+\\frac{5}{8}(i-h) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}h+\\frac{5}{8}i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91ca9633-13a0-5b3f-9d35-131a010c80d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094893} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0f1ec8-8c82-5d98-872e-6808eff3d9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094894} {:answer/id #uuid "40dc8516-4025-5009-a188-9129456e510f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094895} {:answer/id #uuid "5def0dfa-eda1-5673-ac26-4345f4090725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094896} {:answer/id #uuid "658e6611-ce0a-5829-bf6f-1cd2bfcc5908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5def0dfa-eda1-5673-ac26-4345f4090725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094896} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee69212-ba9f-5d68-9bb7-ec1de464bc9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "767cbe5b-8f45-54bf-8422-666ebc7778f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-19338" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/816c4ecbfe49321bc6b2bc0d72eb61d6ddd1aeae7e76a275df0975439dde5865.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/6782f2fa59056afd9022edbaf69c44a991bf12a7ac6a190bea3cc08fc7a4996c.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2.5|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.5b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2.5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2.5b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2.5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80120cea-42f6-536a-a12c-4c3b760859bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 1.5$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094899} {:answer/id #uuid "90ed0152-bc17-5c0a-9fac-769c5a688387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094900} {:answer/id #uuid "b64b31a2-ea40-5bfb-917e-faf58d123194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094901} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5f92d9-4714-55d4-8102-d106e968b4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094902} {:answer/id #uuid "339e78f1-3955-5364-a7de-0d57fddd6b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2.5$ and $μ =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90ed0152-bc17-5c0a-9fac-769c5a688387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094900} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f338b7-0e6c-5006-af37-19877673bf78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b7aee39-22ab-5444-970d-797a67e85565" :question/math-academy-id "q-19370" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/a1e251e00a99520cc392d031dbd05646ce07b65319d829e2cd8fc6f80e6e29a2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/601e049067de307ef2321ae9b516d1c7306d837ca0758c080074446a8c54389d.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=2a+2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "558d0456-17ca-5723-a2a6-0f711767c356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "558d0456-17ca-5723-a2a6-0f711767c356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310744} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b3129e6-0e85-53b4-8d5e-6c29575dc17f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1064db4-89c9-5bbf-882b-ee2e96404aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-275120" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/1d637a841739052b4e2d26f89bbcfd92eefefcebf6000a46a10a45b7e53ecf15.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+3b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/33a5661bc10830c7e52e2ba7f27779a28c6e5f9cf39630e60d0634f3b6f7ccdd.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db866fd-2588-5c42-982c-67d6a3b9a76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244948} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba257bf-af61-5cb7-8b7e-d9463d5e5dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244949} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3c5850-a4fa-5376-9043-a2f592764d44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db866fd-2588-5c42-982c-67d6a3b9a76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244948} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4756c5b-1750-559c-a816-407094d8be46" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3fb92e3-522c-5f79-a155-8a768412d58e" :question/math-academy-id "q-321299" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/6fe3721ff3ab63e75be934c79b03d4ca601513c2e08b987a0bafd94c74856e90.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=a+\\lambda b$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/9c8d67718152bc1bc9569213369580b6fc6f5bc1fe0c63b637f14c9f659dfe27.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{2}{3}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{2}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9baedfb5-b338-52e2-af97-1516e5fe58c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310748} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce7c869-1909-5b29-a677-65749f4689cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9baedfb5-b338-52e2-af97-1516e5fe58c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310748} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc943c4e-d454-5683-a36f-5682f9921a7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c73b0068-3c9b-58bf-bb3d-96edbc18714f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73b0068-3c9b-58bf-bb3d-96edbc18714f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310751} :answer-field/id #uuid "623c8595-5f6a-5c71-834e-fd37420c548e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8871e56f-b40f-5383-aecc-edf37892d8d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-322568" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/7e92a09f8448f4d320f86656bc0774e6167b96156041c32eb7a64ba5e8820128.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OE}=e,$ and $F$ divides $\\overset{―}{DE}$ in the ratio $DF:FE=3:4.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OF}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{DF}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ODE,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\overset{\\to }{DO}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $DF:FE=3:4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\frac{3}{7}\\overset{\\to }{DE}=\\frac{3}{7}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OF} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{3}{7}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}d+\\frac{3}{7}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bfd1f22-b82c-5d60-8942-8eee6131112e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244952} {:answer/id #uuid "a77263ed-2e1b-5871-b785-5b889b9b4ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bfd1f22-b82c-5d60-8942-8eee6131112e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244952} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c13c6d7-7001-5808-b413-3e3a6b9553aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244951} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de8e71e1-33e8-5960-8404-3b41af8a0fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de8e71e1-33e8-5960-8404-3b41af8a0fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244955} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e587f0-cf75-5361-9026-8c0f1f48cac4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a4338d8-16c5-555f-9994-d62335cb5f49" :question/math-academy-id "q-322576" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/36742531a5f9f5d77c5f7e90980120479f4142cecaf6e84937d76d938688409d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=e,$ and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{MN}$ in the ratio $MT:TN=8:3.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OT}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MT:TN=8:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MT}=\\frac{8}{11}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{8}{11}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{8}{11}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{11}d+\\frac{8}{11}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b001ae9-81ad-59c8-8c5b-8cbfd28e8a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b001ae9-81ad-59c8-8c5b-8cbfd28e8a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310754} :answer-field/id #uuid "30c6e253-2c1f-5e7e-9af3-50c7c7d9502d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310753} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf63cd95-a5d1-544a-bb7a-3e6f68873ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf63cd95-a5d1-544a-bb7a-3e6f68873ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310756} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e4272d8-a7bf-5d07-8430-6379ce19a61b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33f4ca89-dab6-5a7c-b774-67f820e3804d" :question/math-academy-id "q-322582" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/76ddb750ca447fd7f73f2481ecd46bb53eba12e392e81e8b64c2c23589e11694.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OK}=k,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=z,$ and $Y$ divides $\\overset{―}{KZ}$ in the ratio $KY:YZ=10:7.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OY}$ via the vectors $k$ and $z.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OY}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$k+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OY}=\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{KY}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OK}=k,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{KY}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OKZ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{KZ} & =\\overset{\\to }{KO}+\\overset{\\to }{OZ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{OZ} \\\\  & =-k+z \\\\  & =z-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $KY:YZ=10:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{KY}=\\frac{10}{17}\\overset{\\to }{KZ}=\\frac{10}{17}(z-k).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OY} & =\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{KY} \\\\  & =k+\\frac{10}{17}(z-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{17}k+\\frac{10}{17}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5101f0dc-70c0-5b62-a2a9-f4da74e5e623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282659} {:answer/id #uuid "b6346d81-b2e7-5fae-a522-68d0fa1ff17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5101f0dc-70c0-5b62-a2a9-f4da74e5e623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282659} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5e7501-56e0-5ddf-8a32-892664f3997c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef999424-e263-53f1-bf60-39690b6d18bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-325983" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/8e6ccf522b5325c3b592ca9470810a95c5491f0b68f000ea774f6555cee0f47f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ then\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/646b8f1322f815e786f82eb8cfb3fa9e84a3c8d588c94910b0b702b607bfb2d8.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{3}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{3}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{3}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{3}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d0f419-57de-547f-8d8e-54d210b142fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310760} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e5b9db-0b7c-56ca-af3e-896e2c8ee0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4e5b9db-0b7c-56ca-af3e-896e2c8ee0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310761} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7be7c3-ef06-563e-90a1-fdd90054876a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ca5f72c-bdbb-5dac-8df8-acc9530dfad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ca5f72c-bdbb-5dac-8df8-acc9530dfad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310763} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fae648c-fad5-586a-9449-3fa74de70bd7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d681eea-b9ef-5eca-b425-7d37983545ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-326013" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/586fb01cd796c9d4e04aaeb393ea7f5fadd6c836d0b39d36c9c668447c490bd1.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/2d3a5c31af776cc49c2af118edc7256ca533320c7d53245593e5de6649ce03cf.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $u$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2v.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-u-2v.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v=-1u-2v.$\n\nSince $v∦u,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a66462c1-78ff-5d0c-8e9d-7113f04b9388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a66462c1-78ff-5d0c-8e9d-7113f04b9388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310766} :answer-field/id #uuid "2350f5b4-d483-51b9-81f0-c41ce94c8b0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310765} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a515de7a-dcd0-5b10-a8b9-7f93b67111c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a515de7a-dcd0-5b10-a8b9-7f93b67111c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310768} :answer-field/id #uuid "535ea4ed-4ac4-537c-ae91-4ecc883b64f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0701942f-40fa-539b-bea9-5c543c7f6d24" :question/math-academy-id "q-326022" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/99d63eb31fba0757bde11dda3653e3791fcddb0b74b5325e20afcede4f3ea849.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/f7acd51dcca9ad48f4aa7ec026beaeb67ddc9b1e88ab9b5042016674ba6ea542.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =a+4b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b=1a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =4$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e879d6-45f0-55a9-a966-627d6a5a481b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310771} {:answer/id #uuid "57a5bc70-f8a6-537e-beb8-e1db288ff4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91e879d6-45f0-55a9-a966-627d6a5a481b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310771} :answer-field/id #uuid "456f1dcc-ef20-59d2-a8c8-6997a0ea7edc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6784d6a1-7329-59ce-9957-b8288b4233b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-327049" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/a43139fc26d5778c40db020a7ebc9122c2fd54c0dcd4eb44cfd1671936a4055a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/1d266a8324d18191f5bc8560be6539d54d1a11916f33ead7432d9f528769fc22.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=\\frac{1}{2}a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98f4480-07ec-5b85-b225-41142f1ac636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244973} {:answer/id #uuid "f96f8ec3-e71e-5a21-b32a-9bc87cb65f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f96f8ec3-e71e-5a21-b32a-9bc87cb65f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244974} :answer-field/id #uuid "01dc1a2d-47f1-56ae-aa52-c4a1cbbf5ea8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244972} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6b644b5-8659-5893-9dbc-fa8fd40213dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6b644b5-8659-5893-9dbc-fa8fd40213dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244976} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe422f1a-a05f-56be-a9b7-8220326b20e4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "963ee32e-9695-5de4-abd4-883ca842b97a" :question/math-academy-id "q-327075" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/e69402e97668c50f91258ca7e8dbb5206d1cd681e73bb9333303777adc866b70.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/cb8209592ec6d5be435622999c1b3eb86e1756cbe9852be0efdbbf41d41777ff.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have  \n\n- $OJ=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $j$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-2j.$  \n\nSimilarly:  \n\n- $JL=|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $k$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=k.$  \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\ni=\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2j+k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j+k.$  \n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way.  Equating the coefficients, we get \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,\\mu =1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b618ba29-f2ef-5ebd-a698-937a01af1adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244979} {:answer/id #uuid "f8112905-742b-5117-9113-54b51ab9f462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244980} {:answer/id #uuid "e8422aa6-e138-5e37-8559-c6815c49b0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244981} {:answer/id #uuid "5da4f64e-9b77-5a80-b4d0-555c64927105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244982} {:answer/id #uuid "bc05fbf6-7334-5828-aeaf-9f8f78233303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8422aa6-e138-5e37-8559-c6815c49b0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244981} :answer-field/id #uuid "406a4f26-b5a6-54fa-a816-81d0fa9bf5ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89f49045-ce3b-55cb-aabe-852c9bd4db2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-367052" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/1d424b5db0534a8d9abc9b0aa84dcb2e6860a7ed51de7390fc4f7e91c1c33f2b.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+4b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+4b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $4b.$ So, we first draw the vector $4b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/d8fdfb800bd9bbb179d51115b86823d99029732fee9810281b9c587a32376993.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $4b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=4|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-4a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =4b-4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+4b=-4a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29336052-c4a9-582d-9220-3ee636684796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=-\\frac{1}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244993} {:answer/id #uuid "3daf1a81-0ba1-59df-8131-cdad3411a73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{1}{3}u+\\frac{2}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244994} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2fc91a-3744-579e-a0f6-950e4a878a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244995} {:answer/id #uuid "b54899df-6d8c-5fd5-a623-369f31e61ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{2}{3}u+\\frac{1}{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244996} {:answer/id #uuid "4f35be66-b00c-5e55-b9b7-7a0b8eb76352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}v-\\frac{2}{9}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d2fc91a-3744-579e-a0f6-950e4a878a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244995} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee483764-dc6f-568b-bd11-a98dda2f857a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2af4a406-e134-5401-990f-61dd08996b3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42669" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/040127594f639248736d4492f1853bcb1e6ef18c37db9242ebe466045713628a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=2:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=2:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{2}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{2}{9}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{2}{9}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f11d0ea-fd73-5946-87fa-d878b132d686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310775} {:answer/id #uuid "7112b026-e694-52c2-b2f4-2df7c5cb133d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310776} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ee48ad-9477-5b06-ad1c-3c46845ff631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310777} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e21439-2e80-57ad-8160-bfa6014380d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310778} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc79b76-8545-5a7b-a148-ef64e525552f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5e21439-2e80-57ad-8160-bfa6014380d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310778} :answer-field/id #uuid "05811b83-cd05-5a22-a3d4-9e629b7f7e3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b8ac28f-007f-5ab1-bbdf-a1098817d26f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70149" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/4b5f0399c2fc40b26dacb6b5c323bb54ba3ecbcf4b4375cdfecc59631140c0da.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/b0b7160eef056eccd922fbd6264cb247b9f787bb431c51ff3345be39aa26b872.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=1.5|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=1.5b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+1.5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+1.5b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1.5\n$$"}]}
